 17592186045417] [17592186490146 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490146 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490146 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7e63c75a-0e87-5ea0-8fd9-4a7908e3c449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490155 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490155 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490155 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490155 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490155 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490155 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490155 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490155 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490155 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8ea1ac60-40da-5758-9bc0-142c36f8f6ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490190 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490190 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490190 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490190 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de313b80-7984-512b-bdaf-802f9aba6f00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490217 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490217 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142538" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490217 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490217 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix with $2$ pivot columns?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=4$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490217 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "128f0273-6319-516f-a4e8-3d58e084f0a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490217 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since $A$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have that $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 2+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490316 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490316 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490316 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490316 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix which has exactly $2$ non-pivot columns?\n\nI. $A$ is  invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=2$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490316 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3a4804f-9ed9-56b0-80c9-162f94bd57af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490316 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has $n=6$ columns and $2$ of them are non-pivot ones, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=6-2=4\\ne 6.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=4$,  then $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=6$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490326 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490326 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490326 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $8\\times 8$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa527559-21f3-5088-ac1e-a7d1439bef18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490326 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =8 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =8-5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490327 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490327 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490327 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490327 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $8\\times 10$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=7$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490327 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb06ba3d-1b9f-5681-af01-0922516167aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490327 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 7+\\text{nullity}(A) & =10 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =10-7 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
